A||||8||L.I. Fieros||A Day At The Drag Strip -- kind of longish||||6||
Z||000000||L.I. Fieros||09-04-2000||09:36 PM||||The Cars:<P>87GT 5spd., 3.1, cam, lowered<P>86GT 4spd., 2.8, bored out TB, air box removed, power pulley kit, Fiero Store headers, lowered, polished heads<P>86SE (my car) 4spd., 2.8 (152K miles), 1.6 rocker arms, ported exhaust manifolds, full MSD Ignition, no CAT, hollow muffler, Hypertech chip<P>    What a day!  My car survived!  It was my first time on the drag strip and it was great!  Today was "Street Legal Day" at the track.  We had three Fieros from our club out there (all Daily Drivers), which made it a lot of fun.  I somehow always wound up running against the low 14 second cars as opposed to the 15-16 second cars though.  On my second run I launched dead even with a guy running 14.2  But as soon as I shifted into second (about 30mph) he drove away from me.  Gave the crowd a good show though!  The very first time I lined up against an IROC.  I'll just follow his lead I guess???  OK, drive up into the burn out area.  Spin the tires, back end walks sideways a bit, CHIRP! We're ready!  Now where the heck are those staging lights???  He's creeping forward a bit so off I go.  No light yet.  Roll forward more.  No light.  Creep forward some more. No light.  Hmmm, it's going to be hard to see the green light through the passenger window???  We were neck and neck at 0.25mph heading down the track!  Oh well.  When we finally got back into place I missed the light and watched him take off before I did anything, and then bogged off the line.  Reaction time, what's that?  But, she still ran a 16.606 @ 79.869 mph and had no problem chirping in second.  Right away we knew today was not going to be the day for low ET's.  It felt like 90 degrees with 100% humidity.  The good thing was that I improved on every run with a 16.589 @ 80.076 mph and finally a 16.522 @ 81.897 mph.  On the final run my launch was not so great but I hung onto third gear through the lights and added about 2mph.  Oh yeah, the other cars.  Brian's 86GT ran a best of 16.55 and Craig's 87GT with the 3.1 liter ran a best of 16.32 @81.689 mph.  Yeah, the 3.1 beat my ET but I had more mph.  By the way, he was running 4 tenths slower than his first time at the track because of the heat.  Next time we're going to make it a club day and do some bracket racing.  Oh, and Fiero's can do NHRA burn outs.  We almost lost sight of Craig's GT behind piles of smoke.  Guess that's about it.<P>One more thing.  What's a good 60 foot time for a V6 Fiero?  My best was 2.241 seconds. Craig pulled 2.100 seconds regularly though.<P>See ya,<BR>Dave<P>||||reg||6
Z||000001||1FST2M6||09-05-2000||12:00 AM||||think your cars need some tuning..  my old 86 SE with 96k on the clock and easy bolt-ons and lots of dyno tuning pulled 14.7 with NO nitrous! it's easyily pull a 3.2 equipped car.. GT Bastard has it now with a bad bearing and it'll STILL out run his friends 3.1 liter car.  get any picks of theose sweet smokey burn outs!! thats'd be a good wallpaper for some of us!<p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 09-05-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||BBBAD GT||09-05-2000||10:34 AM||||Not bad for the first time out.  Please don't get discouraged and never go back, nobody runs what they think they should run the first time out.  You do learn alot just by watching/asking question to people, and you'll find out people like to talk about their cars  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/BBBAD_GT_SIG.gif">    <P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||L.I. Fieros||09-05-2000||06:32 PM||||1FST2M6 -- I think it's more the driver who needs the tuning as opposed to the car in my case.  Your car must be one of those prototype Fieros that escaped from the factory.  Seriously though, a good driver could have taken 1/2 second off my time, but I don't know how much more on that particular day.  My car completely changes when the temp. drops 10 degrees.<P>BBBAD GT -- Thanks.  I can't wait to go back!  To me it was a total success.  I brought this car back from the junkyard.  Took the motor down to the short block, but didn't rebuild it, just cleaned everything up and put all new gaskets (first time for a college kid on a low budget).  So, just the fact that it stayed together and was able to hang with the other Fieros was great.  I also ran it the way I drive it.  I've got some tricks for next time.<P>See ya,<BR>Dave||||reg||1
Z||000004||loafer87gt||09-05-2000||07:21 PM||||1FST2m6, what sort of tuning do you do at your shop? I have never had my car on a dyno before and am curious as to what sort of things a guy can adjust to get the most out of his car. 14.7, thats an awesome time for your car, I've only had my 87 GT out to the track once and ran a 16.6 OUCH!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Shaun41178||09-07-2000||01:33 PM||||Keep practicing and you can probably get those times down into the low 16's.  ice your intake before you race and raise the tire pressure in the front to help minimize rolling resistance.  I have an auto but by icing my intake and raising my tire pressure and unplugging my TC witch I was able to knock off .4 seconds in the qtr.<P>Good luck<P>------------------<BR>Shaun-Toledo OH, 85 GT<BR>3.2 With nitrous<BR>http://members.aol.com/shaun41178||||reg||1
Z||000006||L.I. Fieros||09-07-2000||03:21 PM||||Wow, 4 tenths just from those little things is very encouraging.  You didn't mention removing the spare tire and jack though.  I'm going to try that also.  I'm hoping to break into the 15's because now my dad thinks his Mazda would "blow me away."  The car puts 110hp to the rear wheels (G-Tech) so it should be capable of mid to high 15's on the right day.  Thanks for the info.<P>BTW, how do you ice the intake without making a mess and drawing a crowd?<P>See ya,<BR>Dave||||reg||1
Z||000007||Blade||09-07-2000||05:34 PM||||1fst <BR>what easy bolt ons you sugest?<P>Blade||||reg||1
Z||000008||Shaun41178||09-07-2000||08:22 PM||||I did forget to mention that I did take out my spare tire and jack as well as my passenger seat.  spare tire and jack and seat weigh in at about 60 pounds with the seat weighing the most at around 30 lbs.<P>As far as icing the intake most people at the track do it in the staging lanes and then remove it right before they go to pull up to the staging lights.  Thats what I do.  Yea water drips down but it is in the staging lanes and where I go noone cares.  I thought someone would get upset if water was dripping off my engine but I saw it on other cars too so I figured it was ok and it is.  I just buy a bag of ice from a convenience mart before I leave for the track and pack a little cooler with me to keep the ice in when not using it to keep water out of my trunk.<P>Thre are a few easy bolt ons out there but I guess it depends on how much you want to spend.  I think that by taking out the intake box and air filter box and replacing them with a high flow tube and filter is a good start to help get more air flow into the engine.  I made up my own and picked up 2 hp from it.  Not much but better then nothing.  Getting a larger bore TB is a good bolt on too.  I picked up 4 hp and as much torque throughout the rpm range when I tested it on my 2.8.  Porting the stock exhaust manifolds will give you about 8 as it did to mine and it is a cheap mod too if you do it yourself.  An underdrive pulley gave me about 5 hp and helped get the revs up quicker.  Not much of a hp improvement but I noticed a drop of .2 in the qtr after I did this mod.  Gutting the cat or putting on a larger exhaust like the Borla or just a larger muffler will help in the top end dept.  1.6 ratio rocker arms from comp cams will increase the lift on your cam a bit to get more air in.  That mod is worth about 2-5 hp or so.  I bet there are a few others I missed minus the forced induction route and without tearing the engine down but thats a good start I guess.<P>------------------<BR>Shaun-Toledo OH, 85 GT<BR>3.2 With nitrous<BR>http://members.aol.com/shaun41178||||reg||1
